    {"id": 12, "type": "manual",
     "desc": "Open explanation, part (b). Completing the square turns the equation into (x - 2)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = -7. The student must say what that means for the graph: a sum of two squares of real numbers can never be negative, so no point (x, y) in the plane satisfies the equation and the graph is empty — there is no circle. Accept 'no graph', 'no real points', 'the empty set', or the observation that r^2 = -7 forces an imaginary radius, PROVIDED the student gives the reason (a square is never negative / the left side is always at least 0). Do not accept 'the radius is the square root of -7' with no comment on whether the graph exists, and do not accept 'it is a circle with radius -7'.",
     "skill": "circle-to-standard-form", "facet": "circle-to-standard-form",
     "standard": "HSG-GPE.A.1", "difficulty": 5, "bloom": "justify", "workspace_cm": 8.5}
